    "Balgobin Bhagat was a Kabirpanthi householder-saint — of medium height, fair, above sixty, with greying hair.",
    "His dress was extremely simple — a langoti round the waist, a Kanphati cap like Kabirpanthis, a black kamli in winter, Ramanandi chandan on the forehead and a tulsi rosary round the neck.",
    "He was no sadhu but a householder — son and daughter-in-law, a little farming, a neat house; yet he measured up to every definition of a saint.",
    "He regarded Kabir as 'Sahab', sang Kabir's padas and walked by Kabir's commands.",
    "He never told a lie, kept honest dealings, and never hesitated to speak bluntly.",
    "His honesty was so fine that he never touched another's thing without asking — he would not even sit in another's field for nature's call.",
    "Whatever the field yielded, he carried on his head to the Kabirpanthi math four kos away as 'bhent' (offering); he lived on whatever he received back as 'prasad'.",
    "In Ashadh's drizzle, smeared with mud, he sang Kabir's padas while transplanting paddy — children danced, the lips of women on the ridge trembled, the transplanters' fingers moved in rhythm.",
