    "The lesson 'Khema' is a story by genre; its author is Paa. Pu. Vi. Sa. (Pathyapustak Vikas Samiti) — it is a compiled story.",
    "The story is included in Kislay Bhag-3 and depicts the heart-rending reality of child labour.",
    "The hero of the story is Khema — a small child of eight or nine years who works at Kasara's hotel.",
    "From morning till night, Khema remains busy serving tea to customers, washing glasses and obeying Kasara's orders.",
    "Khema was the son of a poor father; to escape the burden of raising them, the father had sold all four of his children.",
    "Khema was the youngest of the four brothers; Kasara had bought him, which is why he worked at Kasara's hotel.",
    "Working barefoot on the burning ground in summer, Khema asked Kasara for slippers: 'My feet are burning. Get me slippers, uncle.'",
    "On asking for slippers, Kasara scolded him, saying — 'I'll put the slippers on your head right now; go do your work, look who's come asking for slippers.'",
    "Hearing Kasara's scolding, Khema was disheartened; he found relief by pouring water on his burning feet.",
    "Pouring water on his feet gave Khema coolness — a poignant picture of his helplessness and pitiable condition.",
    "The turning point comes when a teacher (the writer) is moved to pity seeing Khema's miserable condition.",
    "The teacher goes to Khema's village, meets his father, and takes the responsibility of taking Khema along and educating him.",
    "When the teacher told Khema's father this, the father's eyes filled with tears — his suppressed affection for his child awoke.",
    "The father's dream was that his son would study and become a great man, but poverty had forced him to strangle his own dreams.",
    "Finally Khema is freed from that hellish life, and the door of education opens for him.",
    "The story leaves behind the grave question of what will become of countless children like Khema, who are still victims of exploitation.",
